---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Blueprints" activeNav="/platform/blueprints">
  <h1>Blueprints</h1>
  <p>
    A blueprint is a reusable contract for an application. It describes the shape of the app's
    metadata JSON, the exact file names the app should carry, and the platforms it should have.
    Applications remain valid records without a blueprint, and a draft blueprint can be attached
    before any of those requirements are filled in.
  </p>
  <Screenshot
    src="blueprints"
    alt="Blueprint detail showing its JSON schema, exact required files, platforms, two conditional hooks, and attached applications"
    caption="One contract brings its requirements, ordered hook actions, and attached applications together."
  />

  <h2>What a blueprint checks</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>How it is matched</th><th>Can be empty?</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>JSON Schema</td>
        <td>Matches the application's metadata JSON against the supported schema keywords</td>
        <td>Yes. An absent schema is treated as a match.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Files</td>
        <td>Exact, case-sensitive file names such as <code>associated-domains.json</code></td>
        <td>Yes. File content, MIME type, and image dimensions are not inspected.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Platforms</td>
        <td>The app has an identifier for each selected platform: iOS, Android, or web</td>
        <td>Yes.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The metadata matcher supports the practical JSON Schema subset used by the console:
    <code>type</code>, <code>required</code>, <code>properties</code>, <code>items</code>,
    <code>enum</code>, <code>const</code>, <code>allOf</code>, <code>anyOf</code>, string length and
    pattern checks, number bounds, and array length checks.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="json" code={`{
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["owner", "release_channel"],
  "properties": {
    "owner": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1 },
    "release_channel": { "enum": ["preview", "production"] }
  }
}`} />

  <h2>Create a draft</h2>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Blueprints</strong> from the workspace sidebar and choose
    <strong>New blueprint</strong>. Only the name is required. You can save the description,
    schema, files, and platforms later, which is useful when the contract is being introduced to
    applications that already exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    Required files are names, not upload fields. Declaring <code>associated-domains.json</code>
    says that attached apps should contain a file with that exact name; it does not attach the file
    to the blueprint or copy it into applications.
  </p>
  <Screenshot
    src="new-blueprint-modal"
    alt="New blueprint form with name, description, optional JSON schema, exact file names, and platform choices"
    caption="Only the name is required; every contract field can be completed later."
  />

  <h2>Attach applications</h2>
  <p>
    An application has at most one blueprint. Open the application, choose <strong>Edit</strong>,
    and select a blueprint. The selection can be changed or cleared at any time. Attaching an
    incomplete application is allowed: the application overview shows which JSON, files, or
    platforms are missing, while the rest of the application remains usable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Removing a blueprint does not remove application metadata, identifiers, files, or tags.
    Deleting a blueprint similarly leaves its applications in place and sets their blueprint to
    none.
  </p>

  <h2>Conditional hooks</h2>
  <p>
    Hooks turn compliance results into tag changes. Each hook contains one <strong>IF</strong>
    condition and one or more ordered actions. A single matching condition can therefore remove an
    old tag and add a new colored or animated tag in the same run.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Condition</th><th>Matches when</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>JSON does not match / matches</td><td>Application metadata fails or passes the blueprint's schema</td></tr>
      <tr><td>File is missing / present</td><td>One exact file declared by this blueprint is absent or present</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Platform is missing / present</td><td>One platform declared by this blueprint has no identifier or has one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Application does not match / matches</td><td>The combined JSON, file, and platform result fails or passes</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Every action is either <strong>Add tags</strong> or <strong>Remove tags</strong>. Tags use the
    same editor as application tags, including hexadecimal colors and the
    <code>pulse</code>, <code>ping</code>, <code>flash</code>, <code>tada</code>, and
    <code>heartbeat</code> animations. Actions run from top to bottom, so order matters when two
    actions mention the same tag.
  </p>
  <Screenshot
    src="new-blueprint-hook-modal"
    alt="New conditional hook form with condition selector and the tag editor open to its color palette and animation choices"
    caption="The hook form reuses the application tag editor, including color and animation. Add action builds an ordered multi-action scenario."
  />

  <h3>Example: move an app from incomplete to ready</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Create a hook with <strong>IF Application matches the blueprint</strong>.</li>
    <li>Add action 1: remove <code>blueprint-incomplete</code>.</li>
    <li>Add action 2: add <code>ready</code> with the desired color and animation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The inverse hook can use <strong>Application does not match the blueprint</strong> to remove
    <code>ready</code> and add <code>blueprint-incomplete</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>When hooks run</h2>
  <p>Attached applications are evaluated when:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>their metadata or blueprint attachment changes;</li>
    <li>a file is uploaded, renamed, or deleted;</li>
    <li>a platform identifier is created, updated, deleted, or synchronized;</li>
    <li>the blueprint or one of its hooks is created or updated; or</li>
    <li>you choose <strong>Evaluate</strong> on the blueprint.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Disabling a hook stops future executions. Deleting a hook deliberately leaves tags already
    written to applications unchanged; use a matching remove action before deletion if they must be
    cleaned up.
  </p>

  <h2>API and CLI</h2>
  <p>
    Blueprint automation uses the independent <code>blueprints:read</code> and
    <code>blueprints:write</code> API key scopes. See
    <a href="/cli/blueprints"><code>appmctl blueprints</code></a> for the domain-aware CLI commands,
    or the <a href="/api-reference">API reference</a> for the complete request schemas.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
